      "sampleAnswer": "The graph illustrates the proportion of American households equipped with three types of technology—personal computers, mobile phones, and internet access—over a five-year period from 1995 to 1999.\n\nOverall, all three technologies experienced significant growth during this period, with mobile phones showing the most dramatic increase. By 1999, mobile phones had become the most widely owned technology among the three categories.\n\nIn 1995, personal computers were present in approximately 30% of US households, making them the most common technology at that time. This figure rose steadily to around 50% by 1999. Internet access, which started at roughly 5% in 1995, grew at a remarkable pace, climbing to approximately 35% by the end of the period.\n\nMobile phone ownership showed the steepest growth trajectory. Starting from about 20% in 1995, the percentage of households with mobile phones surged to nearly 60% in 1999, overtaking personal computer ownership around 1998. This rapid adoption can be attributed to declining handset prices and expanding network coverage during the late 1990s.\n\nIn summary, while all three technologies saw substantial uptake among American households between 1995 and 1999, mobile phones experienced the fastest rate of adoption, transforming from the least popular to the most prevalent technology within just four years."
